      <h2>Research Interests</h2>
      <p>
        My research lies at the intersection of epistemology, AI ethics, and
        philosophy of technology. I am especially interested in how trust,
        dependence, and responsibility are structured in human-AI relations.
        My current work develops a structural account of AI trust that asks
        whether human participants occupy fair and practically inhabitable roles
        within the relevant dependence relation.
      </p>
      <p>
        I also work on collective forgiveness, collective agency, and the
        practical-normative structure of interpersonal and political repair. More
        broadly, I am interested in virtue epistemology, skepticism,
        experimental philosophy, and the methodological status of philosophical
        intuitions.
      </p>

      <h2>Papers in Progress</h2>
      <h3>Unfair Role-Burden Allocation and the Structure of AI Trust</h3>
      <p class="muted">Manuscript in progress.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Develops a structural account of AI trust centered on role allocation in human-AI dependence relations.</li>
        <li>Critiques the governance pipeline from transparency to verification to trustworthiness to trust.</li>
        <li>Argues that fair role distribution is a constitutive condition for rational trust in AI-mediated practices.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Collective Forgiveness as a Thin Practical-Normative Act</h3>
      <p class="muted">Manuscript in progress.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Defends a thin practical account of collective forgiveness against group-mind objections.</li>
        <li>Understands collective forgiveness through standing, recognition, revision, and enactment.</li>
        <li>Examines historical-political forgiveness, institutional continuity, public memory, and narrative self-understanding.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Simplicity and Complexity in Philosophy and Science: A Complementary Perspective</h3>
      <p class="muted">Course paper / work in progress.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Examines simplicity and complexity as complementary methodological principles.</li>
        <li>Critiques overly reductive models while preserving the explanatory value of theoretical simplicity.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Hume on Miracles, Testimony, and Evidential Proportionality</h3>
      <p class="muted">Course paper.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Reconstructs Hume's argument in "Of Miracles" through testimony, evidential proportionality, and comparative probability.</li>
      </ul>

